\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the 403{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $ (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:drift}), against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz to 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} widened TRAPPIST--1{} windows, and it is the window's own ceiling that decides whether a given orbit is contained.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{3}{7}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot \nu /\nu $ (Eq.~\ref {eq:drift}), against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz to \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} widened \AccelWideStar {} windows, and it is the window's own ceiling that decides whether a given orbit is contained}{figure.3}{}}
