\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces EIRP against distance for this survey and for published centimetre-wave searches. Blue: this survey, plotted as $P_{90}^{\rm  sel}$ for the deepest window per star; orange: \citet  {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet  {Margot2023}; violet: \citet  {Mason2025}; left-edge ticks: \citet  {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo 2.38-GHz benchmark, a power scale only. The vertical coordinates are commensurable as total powers for a sub-channel carrier; what differs between programmes is the detection threshold and the completeness level each adopts. This survey's value is a 90 per cent recovery through the complete selection, a stricter convention than the nominal thresholds most of the comparison programmes quote.}}{23}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:contextapp}{{8}{23}{EIRP against distance for this survey and for published centimetre-wave searches. Blue: this survey, plotted as $P_{90}^{\rm sel}$ for the deepest window per star; orange: \citet {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet {Margot2023}; violet: \citet {Mason2025}; left-edge ticks: \citet {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo \ArecGHz -GHz benchmark, a power scale only. The vertical coordinates are commensurable as total powers for a sub-channel carrier; what differs between programmes is the detection threshold and the completeness level each adopts. This survey's value is a 90 per cent recovery through the complete selection, a stricter convention than the nominal thresholds most of the comparison programmes quote}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Two-dimensional sensitivity against window centre frequency for all 1655{} searched windows, symbol by channelisation class, the axis along which completeness differs (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:classcomp}). \emph  {Left axis}: the nominal trigger threshold $P_{\rm  trig}$, an internal detection statistic. \emph  {Right axis}: the effective threshold at the \emph  {median} response, $P_{\rm  eff}=2.08\,P_{\rm  trig}$, which is the power to compare with a transmitter (\S  \ref  {sec:method}); the per-window ratio $P_{\rm  eff,total}/P_{\rm  trig}$ runs $1.33$--$3.63$, so a single window's threshold should be read from the catalogue, not from this axis. Red rings mark the 9{} windows whose smearing correction exceeds 1 per cent; dotted, the Arecibo 2.38-GHz benchmark. Note the two decades of scatter at fixed frequency: there is no single survey sensitivity.}}{23}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sens2d}{{9}{23}{Two-dimensional sensitivity against window centre frequency for all \NWindows {} searched windows, symbol by channelisation class, the axis along which completeness differs (Table~\ref {tab:classcomp}). \emph {Left axis}: the nominal trigger threshold $P_{\rm trig}$, an internal detection statistic. \emph {Right axis}: the effective threshold at the \emph {median} response, $P_{\rm eff}=\HanFacMed \,P_{\rm trig}$, which is the power to compare with a transmitter (\S \ref {sec:method}); the per-window ratio $P_{\rm eff,total}/P_{\rm trig}$ runs $\PeffRatioLo $--$\PeffRatioHi $, so a single window's threshold should be read from the catalogue, not from this axis. Red rings mark the \NSmearLo {} windows whose smearing correction exceeds 1 per cent; dotted, the Arecibo \ArecGHz -GHz benchmark. Note the two decades of scatter at fixed frequency: there is no single survey sensitivity}{figure.9}{}}
