\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Exchangeability of the 512-position control ensemble by stratum, over 1655{} windows. Boxes give the per-window control maximum, black diamonds the median on-star peak, and $p$ the Kolmogorov--Smirnov test of the per-window rank against $U(0,1)$. \emph  {(a)} by ALMA band, with the window count under each label; \emph  {(b)} by channelisation class, fine ($<5$\,MHz) against coarse. The ensemble tracks the on-star statistic in every band, and the fine class sits higher than the coarse, 5.77{} against 4.44, as its larger trial count predicts. The fine class departs from uniformity, $D=0.075$ ($\pv  {=0.020}$); the coarse class does not, $D=0.015$ ($\pv  {=0.932}$). The departure is not the flagged windows' doing --- removing all 52{} fine crossings lowers the statistic only to 0.087{} --- and \S  \ref  {sec:radial} identifies the radial noise gradient as the cause.}}{35}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ctrldiag}{{13}{35}{Exchangeability of the \NCtrl -position control ensemble by stratum, over \NWindows {} windows. Boxes give the per-window control maximum, black diamonds the median on-star peak, and $p$ the Kolmogorov--Smirnov test of the per-window rank against $U(0,1)$. \emph {(a)} by ALMA band, with the window count under each label; \emph {(b)} by channelisation class, fine ($<5$\,MHz) against coarse. The ensemble tracks the on-star statistic in every band, and the fine class sits higher than the coarse, \CtrlMedFine {} against \CtrlMedCoarse , as its larger trial count predicts. The fine class departs from uniformity, $D=\KsFineD $ ($\pv {\KsFineP }$); the coarse class does not, $D=\KsCoarseD $ ($\pv {\KsCoarseP }$). The departure is not the flagged windows' doing --- removing all \KsFineCrossN {} fine crossings lowers the statistic only to \KsFineNoCrossD {} --- and \S \ref {sec:radial} identifies the radial noise gradient as the cause}{figure.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces The stage-1 star--band pairs, scored under both statistics. $S_{\rm  reg}$: region maximum of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar} over $n_{\rm  src}=135$ positions; $T_\star $: the symmetric single-position statistic; `ctrl.\ max': the largest control statistic, which is the same ensemble under either. All 5{} are flagged by the symmetric statistic; that is how the rows were chosen. $\nu $ is each window's \emph  {centre}; the crossing frequency differs by up to 0.85\,GHz and Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:ledger} carries $\nu _{\rm  cross}$. For CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two coincide. The region-maximum statistic promotes 34{} further star--band pairs that the symmetric statistic does not, and 0{} in the other direction.}}{35}{table.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bothstats}{{21}{35}{The stage-1 star--band pairs, scored under both statistics. $S_{\rm reg}$: region maximum of Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar} over $n_{\rm src}=135$ positions; $T_\star $: the symmetric single-position statistic; `ctrl.\ max': the largest control statistic, which is the same ensemble under either. All \BothStatsRows {} are flagged by the symmetric statistic; that is how the rows were chosen. $\nu $ is each window's \emph {centre}; the crossing frequency differs by up to 0.85\,GHz and Table~\ref {tab:ledger} carries $\nu _{\rm cross}$. For CP$-$72~2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two coincide. The region-maximum statistic promotes \RegOnlyPairs {} further star--band pairs that the symmetric statistic does not, and \SymOnlyPairs {} in the other direction}{table.21}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces The survey rescored under both repairs. ``Survives'' is the largest stellar debit $m(0)$, in units of each window's own control scale, at which the star still exceeds every detrended control; it is a property of the window and assumes neither debit. For reference the conservative debit is 0.15$\,\sigma $ and the debit that centres the calibration-sample rank distribution is 0.32$\,\sigma $, and a window survives whichever debit is smaller than its entry. No window is added by either repair.}}{35}{table.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:repaired}{{22}{35}{The survey rescored under both repairs. ``Survives'' is the largest stellar debit $m(0)$, in units of each window's own control scale, at which the star still exceeds every detrended control; it is a property of the window and assumes neither debit. For reference the conservative debit is \RmDebitCons $\,\sigma $ and the debit that centres the calibration-sample rank distribution is \RmDebitCal $\,\sigma $, and a window survives whichever debit is smaller than its entry. No window is added by either repair}{table.22}{}}
