\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces The external calibration sample as searched at 2026 September 17. \emph  {(a)} what the frozen pipeline returned on 1322{} windows it had never seen. \emph  {(b)} the 2{} unattributed outliers, all of them from the survey sample, each a single cell a few per cent above the largest of 512{} controls, and none recurring at its own tuning in a further block. The repeat row gives the largest statistic the further block returns anywhere on its own grid; the \emph  {pinned} value at the first block's own frequency and drift, which is the stronger test, is 3.60{} for CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 (\S  \ref  {sec:cp72}).}}{37}{table.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:heldout}{{26}{37}{The external calibration sample as searched at \CampAsOf . \emph {(a)} what the frozen pipeline returned on \CampWindows {} windows it had never seen. \emph {(b)} the \NStageOneUnattrib {} unattributed outliers, all of them from the survey sample, each a single cell a few per cent above the largest of \NCtrl {} controls, and none recurring at its own tuning in a further block. The repeat row gives the largest statistic the further block returns anywhere on its own grid; the \emph {pinned} value at the first block's own frequency and drift, which is the stronger test, is \CpRecT {} for CP$-$72~2713 (\S \ref {sec:cp72})}{table.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Why the spatial control ensemble is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}add-one rank of the star among its 512{} controls, for the survey and for the calibration windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph  {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S  \ref  {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $0.14\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median 0.0032$\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$).}}{38}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rankcdf}{{14}{38}{Why the spatial control ensemble is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph {(a)}~add-one rank of the star among its \NCtrl {} controls, for the survey and for the calibration windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S \ref {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph {(b)}~the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $\RadRIn \,\theta _{\rm PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median \RadUStarMed $\,\theta _{\rm PB}$)}{figure.14}{}}
